    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d7">

                <pb facs="0121" n="9" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg9"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.71 Manchuria/78: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-10T14:00:00+08:00">January 10, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 10—5:33 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>12. Department’s telegram 2, January 4, 6 p.m. Local Foreign Commissioner of
                    Chinese Posts states that ordinary mail service between China and “Manchukuo”
                    began today. Ordinary Chinese postage stamps are being used in China. Consul
                    General at Harbin<anchor n="13" xml:id="d7-f1"/> reports that special postage
                    stamps bearing no personal image nor name of country but only the amount, the
                    two Chinese characters meaning Postal Administration, and an orchid, emblem now
                    used by Pu Yi,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d7-f2"/> are on sale for mail matter for
                    transmission to China. No parcel post or money order service yet
                    established.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d7-f1" n="13" xml:id="d7-f1-content">
